text:
{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "To increase sales at local stores by encouraging residents to buy new products.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text clearly states the café helps people repair old items instead of buying new ones, which directly contradicts the idea of increasing sales at local stores. The shopkeeper even complains about declining sales, not increased ones."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To provide free job training for unemployed residents in repair and sewing skills.",  
      "why_wrong": "While volunteers share skills, the text never mentions job training or employment as a goal. The focus is on repairing items and reducing waste, not on workforce development or employment programs."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To replace the town’s waste disposal system with a volunteer-run recycling program.",  
      "why_wrong": "The café does not replace the town’s waste disposal system. It reduces waste by repairing items, but the text does not suggest any change to the official waste management infrastructure."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To help the town council save money on public events and community programs.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text says the café saved money on waste disposal costs, not that it helped the council save on events or programs. There is no mention of funding public events or reducing costs in that area."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To attract tourists to Millbrook by promoting unique community activities.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text does not mention tourism or any effort to attract visitors. The Repair Café is described as a local, community-based initiative focused on residents and sustainability, not on drawing outside crowds."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To replace the need for local shops by offering all household repairs for free.",  
      "why_wrong": "The café does not aim to replace local shops. It supports reuse, but the text shows it coexists with local businesses—Ms. Whitmore even owns a store that sells household goods, and her concern is about competition, not replacement."  
    }  
  ]  
}
